import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestHeader;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class StraitWebhookController {
    private final String secret = System.getenv("WEBHOOK_SECRET");

    // byte[] gets the raw body; a String or an object would not match the signature.
    @PostMapping("/webhooks/strait")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> receive(@RequestBody byte[] body,
                                        @RequestHeader(value = "X-Strait-Signature", required = false) String signature) {
        if (!StraitSignature.verify(secret, body, signature)) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(401).build();
        }
        // parse body with your JSON library, then handle event / data
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }
}
```

```go title="Go: net/http handler"
package webhooks

import (
	"io"
	"net/http"
	"os"
	"time"
)

func Handler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	body, err := io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(r.Body, 1<<20))
	if err != nil || !VerifyStraitSignature(os.Getenv("WEBHOOK_SECRET"), body, r.Header.Get("X-Strait-Signature"), time.Now()) {
		http.Error(w, "invalid signature", http.StatusUnauthorized)
		return
	}
	// json.Unmarshal(body, &event); handle event.Event / event.Data
	w.WriteHeader(http.StatusNoContent)
}
```

For Django, use `request.body`. For FastAPI, `await request.body()`. Reply within 5 seconds, then do slow work in a queue.

**You should see:** a request to your endpoint with a made-up header, such as `curl -X POST https://your-server/webhooks/strait -H "X-Strait-Signature: t=1,v1=00" -d '{}'`, gets `401`.

A URL button can end in one variable. Meta's rules for URL buttons: "Templates are limited to two URL buttons", the URL "Supports 1 variable, appended to the end of the URL string", the URL is "2000 characters maximum" and the button label is "25 characters maximum" ([Meta: Template components](https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/business-messaging/whatsapp/templates/components)). Set the button URL to your link domain followed by <code>&#123;&#123;1&#125;&#125;</code>, and the slug becomes the variable:

```bash
curl "https://graph.facebook.com/v23.0/$WABA_ID/message_templates" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $WHATSAPP_TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "name": "festive_sale",
    "language": "en",
    "category": "MARKETING",
    "parameter_format": "positional",
    "components": [
      {
        "type": "body",
        "text": "Hi &#123;&#123;1&#125;&#125;, our festive sale is live. Tap below to open it in the app.",
        "example": { "body_text": [["Asha"]] }
      },
      {
        "type": "buttons",
        "buttons": [
          {
            "type": "url",
            "text": "Open the sale",
            "url": "https://hilltop.strait.link/&#123;&#123;1&#125;&#125;",
            "example": ["https://hilltop.strait.link/diwali"]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }'
```

This follows Meta's [custom marketing template](https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/business-messaging/whatsapp/templates/marketing-templates/custom-marketing-templates) request. Use your WhatsApp Business account ID as `WABA_ID` and the Graph API version your Meta app uses. You can create the same template in WhatsApp Manager instead.

**You should see:** `{"id": "…", "status": "PENDING", "category": "MARKETING"}`, then the template turning **Active** (`APPROVED`) in WhatsApp Manager once Meta has reviewed it.

Meta requires opt-in before you message anyone: businesses "are required to obtain opt-in permission" and must name the business people are opting in to ([Meta: Get opt-in for WhatsApp](https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/business-messaging/whatsapp/getting-opt-in)). Send the template with the [Messages API](https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/business-messaging/whatsapp/reference/whatsapp-business-phone-number/message-api). The button's `parameters` hold only the slug, not the whole URL:

```js title="Node: send-template.mjs"
// Node 18+. WHATSAPP_TOKEN and PHONE_NUMBER_ID come from your Meta app.
export async function sendCampaign(to, firstName, slug) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://graph.facebook.com/v23.0/${process.env.PHONE_NUMBER_ID}/messages`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.WHATSAPP_TOKEN}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      messaging_product: 'whatsapp',
      recipient_type: 'individual',
      to, // e.g. '919876543210'
      type: 'template',
      template: {
        name: 'festive_sale',
        language: { code: 'en' },
        components: [
          { type: 'body', parameters: [{ type: 'text', text: firstName }] },
          { type: 'button', sub_type: 'url', index: '0', parameters: [{ type: 'text', text: slug }] },
        ],
      },
    }),
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`WhatsApp send failed: HTTP ${res.status} ${await res.text()}`);
  return res.json();
}
```

